1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:05,000
As big as a battleship, as small as a backpack.

2
00:00:05,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Dispensers of comfort.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
By harnessing the power of fire and ice,

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:12,000
these machines have given us the ability

5
00:00:12,001 --> 00:00:16,000
to create man-made weather and change the world.

6
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Now, heating and air conditioning on Modern Marvels.

7
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:44,000
The Mojave Desert, a barren wasteland.

8
00:00:45,940 --> 00:00:51,160
For centuries, the exclusive domain of cactus and rattlesnakes.

9
00:00:51,260 --> 00:00:54,800
Today, in the middle of the Mojave rests the ultimate boom

10
00:00:54,900 --> 00:00:56,660
town, Las Vegas.

11
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:03,000
The destruction of a hotel like the Sands is a rite of passage

12
00:01:03,100 --> 00:01:05,240
in Las Vegas.

13
00:01:05,340 --> 00:01:09,000
Here on a regular basis, a smaller, older building

14
00:01:09,100 --> 00:01:11,280
will come down in a cloud of dust

15
00:01:11,380 --> 00:01:12,720
to make way for a larger one.

16
00:01:15,880 --> 00:01:19,060
Three years and $1.5 billion later,

17
00:01:19,160 --> 00:01:21,960
the Venetian Hotel opened where the Sands once stood.

18
00:01:25,260 --> 00:01:29,100
The Venetian, which is the size of 104 football fields,

19
00:01:29,101 --> 00:01:31,400
is just one of dozens of towering buildings

20
00:01:31,500 --> 00:01:33,800
that have turned this patch of desert

21
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:37,600
into the ultimate gambling oasis.

22
00:01:37,700 --> 00:01:40,340
But none of this would exist without heating and air

23
00:01:40,440 --> 00:01:42,740
conditioning.

24
00:01:42,840 --> 00:01:45,980
There are few ideas in history that

25
00:01:46,080 --> 00:01:49,360
have had such a great overall impact on the world

26
00:01:49,460 --> 00:01:53,760
and the way that we do things and the way that we live.

27
00:01:53,860 --> 00:01:56,600
And living in Las Vegas means keeping cool in a city

28
00:01:56,601 --> 00:02:02,840
where outdoor temperatures often top 110 degrees.

29
00:02:02,940 --> 00:02:05,040
But thanks to heating and air conditioning,

30
00:02:05,140 --> 00:02:07,240
you can bet the temperature inside the Venetian

31
00:02:07,340 --> 00:02:13,760
will always remain a comfortable 70 degrees.

32
00:02:13,860 --> 00:02:15,560
Surprisingly, the process for cooling

33
00:02:15,660 --> 00:02:19,860
begins outside in the scorching desert heat.

34
00:02:19,960 --> 00:02:22,900
High-powered fans like these stationed around the Venetian

35
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:26,600
draw hot air inside.

36
00:02:26,601 --> 00:02:31,080
What will turn this hot air cold are 10 massive 2,000 ton

37
00:02:31,180 --> 00:02:33,840
machines called chillers.

38
00:02:33,940 --> 00:02:36,340
They are housed inside the casino's mini processing

39
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:40,280
plant, nicknamed the heart of the house.

40
00:02:40,380 --> 00:02:42,420
A chiller is kind of the reverse of a boiler.

41
00:02:42,520 --> 00:02:45,080
If you think of a boiler that heats up and makes hot water

42
00:02:45,180 --> 00:02:47,820
to distribute around, the chiller makes cold water

43
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:49,020
to distribute around.

44
00:02:49,120 --> 00:02:51,400
That cold water is usually somewhere in the neighborhood

45
00:02:51,500 --> 00:02:52,920
of 40 to 45 degrees.

46
00:02:52,921 --> 00:02:58,920
The key to keeping the water cold are refrigeration

47
00:02:58,921 --> 00:03:00,920
chemicals.

48
00:03:00,921 --> 00:03:04,920
Like most modern-day systems, the one at the Venetian uses an

49
00:03:04,921 --> 00:03:10,920
environmentally friendly chemical called 134A, which is

50
00:03:10,921 --> 00:03:16,920
replacing freon and other ozone-damaging chlorofluorocarbons.

51
00:03:16,921 --> 00:03:21,920
That family of compounds would gradually migrate up to the

52
00:03:21,921 --> 00:03:25,260
levels of the atmosphere and reduce the amount of ozone

53
00:03:25,360 --> 00:03:26,800
in the upper atmosphere.

54
00:03:26,900 --> 00:03:30,800
The ozone is something that does help shield us from ultraviolet

55
00:03:30,900 --> 00:03:33,800
radiation from the sun.

56
00:03:33,900 --> 00:03:37,240
Refrigeration chemicals easily change from a liquid to a gas

57
00:03:37,340 --> 00:03:39,300
and back to a liquid.

58
00:03:39,400 --> 00:03:42,280
And in the process of changing from a liquid to a gas,

59
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:45,540
they absorb a tremendous amount of heat.

60
00:03:45,640 --> 00:03:47,980
That heat comes from water in close proximity

61
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:51,920
to the refrigeration pipes inside the chillers.

62
00:03:51,921 --> 00:03:55,520
The result is colder water.

63
00:03:55,620 --> 00:03:59,220
That cold water is then piped to a network of coils housed

64
00:03:59,320 --> 00:04:01,560
inside air intake units.

65
00:04:01,660 --> 00:04:03,860
Cooled water chills these coils.

66
00:04:03,960 --> 00:04:07,460
Air is blown across that water coil with a fan and then back

67
00:04:07,560 --> 00:04:08,680
to the chiller.

68
00:04:08,780 --> 00:04:11,580
The air is then sent down the ductwork after it's been

69
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:13,580
filtered, and that air is delivered to the rooms

70
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:15,680
through the ceiling.

71
00:04:15,780 --> 00:04:18,980
Ensuring that more than 60,000 guests each day

72
00:04:18,981 --> 00:04:23,480
can gamble in climate-controlled comfort.

73
00:04:23,580 --> 00:04:26,580
When it's cold outside, the same process is used for heating

74
00:04:26,680 --> 00:04:27,720
the building.

75
00:04:27,820 --> 00:04:31,220
Only boilers do the work of chillers.

76
00:04:31,320 --> 00:04:34,720
A boiler is a large steel drum that contains water.

77
00:04:34,820 --> 00:04:38,240
A natural gas unit provides the fire that heats the water to

78
00:04:38,340 --> 00:04:43,240
well over 200 degrees. This hot water produces steam

79
00:04:43,340 --> 00:04:46,680
that is pumped through the pipes to the coils that warm

80
00:04:46,681 --> 00:04:52,480
that warm the incoming air. Technology has allowed us to

81
00:04:52,580 --> 00:04:56,420
control our indoor environment. But with all its modern day

82
00:04:56,520 --> 00:04:59,420
sophistication, the roots of heating and air conditioning

83
00:04:59,520 --> 00:05:05,340
date back to two of Earth's ancient elements, fire and ice.

84
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:11,840
Campfires were the earliest and only heating systems for thousands

85
00:05:11,940 --> 00:05:14,440
of years. The evolution

86
00:05:14,441 --> 00:05:19,180
from a simple campfire out in the open to the idea of bringing

87
00:05:19,280 --> 00:05:22,440
the campfire inside.

88
00:05:22,540 --> 00:05:26,080
Fire warmed caves and huts for centuries.

89
00:05:26,180 --> 00:05:30,160
Around 2500 BC, the hearth was used by the Greeks to heat

90
00:05:30,260 --> 00:05:32,160
rooms.

91
00:05:32,260 --> 00:05:35,760
And about 1,000 years later, the Egyptians introduced

92
00:05:35,860 --> 00:05:41,260
the bellows to fan the flames and enhance the heat of fires.

93
00:05:41,261 --> 00:05:46,260
But central heating remained elusive until around 100 BC

94
00:05:46,261 --> 00:05:49,760
with the rise of the Roman Empire and their hypocaust

95
00:05:49,761 --> 00:05:54,760
system. The first central heating system

96
00:05:54,761 --> 00:05:57,760
ever developed, it required that homes and buildings be

97
00:05:57,761 --> 00:06:01,760
constructed with raised floors that rested on sturdy ceramic

98
00:06:01,761 --> 00:06:05,760
tile pillars. The shallow basements created by

99
00:06:05,761 --> 00:06:08,760
these raised floors were connected to furnaces where

100
00:06:08,761 --> 00:06:13,760
was burned. During the next few centuries,

101
00:06:13,761 --> 00:06:16,760
Romans continued to improve the hypocaust heating system,

102
00:06:16,761 --> 00:06:19,760
modifying it so that the wood-burning furnace was outside

103
00:06:19,761 --> 00:06:22,760
the building. The heat from the furnace would

104
00:06:22,761 --> 00:06:25,760
rise through a metal pipe connected from the furnace to

105
00:06:25,761 --> 00:06:28,760
the building. They got to the point where

106
00:06:28,761 --> 00:06:32,760
they brought the hot air and flue gases of the fire underneath

107
00:06:32,761 --> 00:06:35,760
the floor of the building and actually up through the walls

108
00:06:35,761 --> 00:06:38,760
themselves, so you would have not only a heated floor, but you

109
00:06:38,761 --> 00:06:43,360
also had heated walls as well. The buildings were made of

110
00:06:43,361 --> 00:06:47,200
stone, which is very good at retaining heat.

111
00:06:47,201 --> 00:06:51,200
But despite the HypoCost system's effectiveness, the concept of

112
00:06:51,201 --> 00:06:54,720
heating an entire structure from one source was forgotten with the

113
00:06:54,721 --> 00:06:59,520
fall of the Roman Empire. For many centuries, heating

114
00:06:59,521 --> 00:07:02,720
returned to building campfires indoors.

115
00:07:02,721 --> 00:07:05,720
Only this time, the campfires were housed inside a structure

116
00:07:05,820 --> 00:07:09,420
known as a fireplace.

117
00:07:09,520 --> 00:07:11,720
Wood was still used as fuel.

118
00:07:11,820 --> 00:07:13,760
Families had to huddle close to the fireplace

119
00:07:13,860 --> 00:07:16,460
to enjoy its warmth. As much of the heat

120
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:19,880
went right up the chimney with the smoke.

121
00:07:19,980 --> 00:07:23,780
In the 1400s, the Germans came up with a better solution,

122
00:07:23,880 --> 00:07:28,720
the metal heating stove. Using wood as fuel,

123
00:07:28,820 --> 00:07:31,720
it radiated heat in all directions.

124
00:07:31,721 --> 00:07:35,760
You could actually contain the fire totally inside the stove,

125
00:07:35,761 --> 00:07:40,160
so you had a much more efficient and controllable means of heating

126
00:07:40,161 --> 00:07:43,360
with a stove than you did with a fireplace.

127
00:07:43,361 --> 00:07:48,160
Despite their popularity, stoves could only heat one room.

128
00:07:48,161 --> 00:07:51,080
It would take a revolution to change all that,

129
00:07:51,081 --> 00:07:52,880
the Industrial Revolution.

130
00:07:52,881 --> 00:07:55,680
As we hit the Industrial Revolution,

131
00:07:55,681 --> 00:07:59,680
that's really what gave a kick in the pants to the idea of really

132
00:07:59,681 --> 00:08:03,480
really developing true central heating systems as we now know

133
00:08:03,481 --> 00:08:05,780
them.

134
00:08:05,781 --> 00:08:08,320
In the early 1800s, American inventor

135
00:08:08,321 --> 00:08:11,720
Daniel Pettibone examined diagrams of the ruins of the

136
00:08:11,721 --> 00:08:15,100
Roman Empire and discovered their sophisticated hypocaust

137
00:08:15,101 --> 00:08:17,860
heating systems.

138
00:08:17,861 --> 00:08:21,100
Inspired by the Roman system, Pettibone designed one of the

139
00:08:21,101 --> 00:08:26,100
first successful modern-day central heating systems.

140
00:08:26,101 --> 00:08:30,100
He built a brick chamber with a stove inside.

141
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:32,540
Cold air entered the chamber at the bottom,

142
00:08:32,640 --> 00:08:35,240
was heated by the stove, and this warm air

143
00:08:35,340 --> 00:08:37,340
rose through ducts in the walls of the rooms.

144
00:08:39,940 --> 00:08:42,420
The system, which was installed at Pennsylvania Hospital

145
00:08:42,520 --> 00:08:47,080
in Philadelphia, relied on heat naturally rising.

146
00:08:47,180 --> 00:08:49,020
So there was no consistency in temperature

147
00:08:49,120 --> 00:08:51,460
from one room to the next.

148
00:08:51,560 --> 00:08:55,300
Engineers solved that problem using two new inventions.

149
00:08:55,301 --> 00:08:57,640
You were able to take a mechanical fan, power that

150
00:08:57,740 --> 00:09:00,700
with a steam engine, and then use that air to ventilate

151
00:09:00,800 --> 00:09:03,240
and heat a building.

152
00:09:03,340 --> 00:09:06,440
Forced air heat had arrived.

153
00:09:06,540 --> 00:09:09,440
The source was a stove or furnace.

154
00:09:09,540 --> 00:09:11,880
And the fuel was either wood or coal,

155
00:09:11,980 --> 00:09:14,220
which came into widespread use in America

156
00:09:14,320 --> 00:09:16,760
with the opening of the first commercial coal mines

157
00:09:16,860 --> 00:09:18,260
during the 1740s.

158
00:09:21,500 --> 00:09:23,800
Throughout the first half of the 19th century,

159
00:09:23,801 --> 00:09:27,840
The demand for central heating grew, first in industry, then

160
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:31,000
homes.

161
00:09:31,100 --> 00:09:35,140
A key component in this heating system was a boiler, which

162
00:09:35,240 --> 00:09:41,660
worked on the same principle as those at the Venetian.

163
00:09:41,760 --> 00:09:44,660
A boiler in the basement heated water, which rose through pipes

164
00:09:44,760 --> 00:09:47,960
either in the form of hot water or steam, depending on the design

165
00:09:48,060 --> 00:09:51,860
of the system.

166
00:09:51,861 --> 00:09:55,860
The pipes were connected to a radiator in each room.

167
00:09:55,960 --> 00:09:59,260
The radiator was a large iron structure with cavities

168
00:09:59,360 --> 00:10:01,760
through which hot water or steam would circulate.

169
00:10:05,100 --> 00:10:08,000
The steam or hot water warmed the iron radiator,

170
00:10:08,100 --> 00:10:10,920
producing heat.

171
00:10:11,020 --> 00:10:15,520
By the early 1900s, radiators became the rage.

172
00:10:15,620 --> 00:10:18,820
We had radiators that could go around columns of buildings.

173
00:10:18,920 --> 00:10:21,220
We had radiators that could go in corners.

174
00:10:21,221 --> 00:10:24,820
We had stair-step radiators that you could put under the stairs.

175
00:10:24,920 --> 00:10:28,020
We even had radiators with warming compartments in them

176
00:10:28,120 --> 00:10:30,560
with ornate doors that could be put in the dining room.

177
00:10:30,660 --> 00:10:33,000
And so you could put your food in these things

178
00:10:33,100 --> 00:10:36,100
and keep it warm while you were having your first course

179
00:10:36,200 --> 00:10:38,440
of your meal.

180
00:10:38,540 --> 00:10:41,140
Regardless of what system people chose to install,

181
00:10:41,240 --> 00:10:43,800
they all shared a common problem.

182
00:10:43,900 --> 00:10:47,140
Much of the heat was escaping from the building.

183
00:10:47,240 --> 00:10:50,480
The law of thermodynamics says that heat always moves

184
00:10:50,481 --> 00:10:52,180
in a direction from warmer to colder.

185
00:10:52,280 --> 00:10:55,320
In the wintertime, you have the condition of colder outside,

186
00:10:55,420 --> 00:10:58,660
so the heat that's in can go out very easily.

187
00:10:58,760 --> 00:11:00,620
To keep the heat indoors, engineers

188
00:11:00,720 --> 00:11:03,360
developed insulation.

189
00:11:03,460 --> 00:11:08,840
Early attempts included corn husks, sawdust, and pine cones.

190
00:11:08,940 --> 00:11:12,000
In the late 1800s, people ground up old newspapers,

191
00:11:12,100 --> 00:11:16,800
treated it with fire retardant, and stuffed it in the walls.

192
00:11:16,900 --> 00:11:18,840
Unfortunately, these early insulators

193
00:11:18,841 --> 00:11:22,840
attracted unwelcome house guests, rodents.

194
00:11:26,740 --> 00:11:29,780
Finally, in the early 1930s, engineers found a way

195
00:11:29,880 --> 00:11:33,880
to take compact strands of tiny glass fibers, melt them,

196
00:11:33,980 --> 00:11:38,600
spin them like cotton candy, and create fiberglass insulation,

197
00:11:38,700 --> 00:11:40,600
today's standard.

198
00:11:40,700 --> 00:11:43,640
Insulation is very important when creating a system.

199
00:11:43,740 --> 00:11:46,440
Remember, heat naturally flows in the direction

200
00:11:46,540 --> 00:11:47,540
from hot to cold.

201
00:11:47,541 --> 00:11:50,840
The more insulation you have, the slower it is for anything

202
00:11:50,940 --> 00:11:53,080
to get through.

203
00:11:53,180 --> 00:11:55,080
Another insulation gained popularity

204
00:11:55,180 --> 00:12:00,580
in the early 1900s, asbestos.

205
00:12:00,680 --> 00:12:04,160
Asbestos is a mineral comprised of microscopic fire retardant

206
00:12:04,260 --> 00:12:06,260
fibers.

207
00:12:06,360 --> 00:12:09,000
It's very effective for holding in heat.

208
00:12:09,100 --> 00:12:11,560
But if these particles should break away from the insulation

209
00:12:11,660 --> 00:12:17,000
and become airborne, they can embed in a person's lungs.

210
00:12:17,001 --> 00:12:21,800
Asbestos can cause cancer. It stopped being used in the 1970s

211
00:12:21,801 --> 00:12:27,140
and 1980s. Insulation helped contain heat,

212
00:12:27,141 --> 00:12:30,140
but maintaining a consistent room temperature had always been a

213
00:12:30,141 --> 00:12:33,880
problem. Coal was the primary fuel, and

214
00:12:33,881 --> 00:12:38,480
the only way to get coal into a furnace was to shovel it, which

215
00:12:38,481 --> 00:12:41,800
meant somebody had to be around day or night to stoke the coals

216
00:12:41,801 --> 00:12:47,000
when the fire died down. That changed with the

217
00:12:47,001 --> 00:12:49,400
electricity. Once we had electric power,

218
00:12:49,401 --> 00:12:53,080
then we could use a thermostat to actually control an electric

219
00:12:53,081 --> 00:12:57,080
current to turn an electric motor off and on.

220
00:12:57,081 --> 00:12:59,720
The thermostat became an integral part of central

221
00:12:59,721 --> 00:13:02,760
heating. Mounted on a wall, it was connected

222
00:13:02,761 --> 00:13:06,440
by wires to a motor attached to the furnace.

223
00:13:06,441 --> 00:13:10,360
As the temperature dropped inside a house, it sent out an electrical signal

224
00:13:10,361 --> 00:13:15,240
to the motor to start the furnace.

225
00:13:15,241 --> 00:13:18,840
Along with electricity and thermostats came new fuels,

226
00:13:18,841 --> 00:13:22,840
oil and natural gas. This combination ignited the

227
00:13:22,841 --> 00:13:27,080
development of central heating and forever vanquished the bite

228
00:13:27,081 --> 00:13:33,160
of winter. For centuries, this was one of

229
00:13:33,161 --> 00:13:37,320
the few escapes from summer heat. And if you didn't live near

230
00:13:37,321 --> 00:13:41,400
water, you found the nearest shady spot.

231
00:13:41,401 --> 00:13:44,760
The technology of keeping cool lagged far behind that of staying

232
00:13:44,761 --> 00:13:49,000
warm. In ancient Egypt, fans were used

233
00:13:49,001 --> 00:13:53,760
by servants to cool their royal masters.

234
00:13:53,761 --> 00:13:58,160
Many cultures developed fans that could fit in a person's hand,

235
00:13:58,161 --> 00:14:02,520
but they didn't provide much relief.

236
00:14:02,521 --> 00:14:05,160
Keeping the masses cool didn't become a real concern

237
00:14:05,161 --> 00:14:09,860
until the rise of factories. During the summer, many of them

238
00:14:09,861 --> 00:14:13,000
were like sweat boxes, devastating to workers

239
00:14:13,001 --> 00:14:16,500
and economically disastrous to many of the products being made.

240
00:14:19,340 --> 00:14:21,800
In candy manufacturing, if there was too much humidity

241
00:14:21,801 --> 00:14:23,940
in the factory, the candy or the chocolates

242
00:14:23,941 --> 00:14:27,040
would stick together, and the yarns and the wools

243
00:14:27,041 --> 00:14:28,280
would expand and contract.

244
00:14:28,281 --> 00:14:30,820
And it was very hard from one day to the next

245
00:14:30,821 --> 00:14:32,660
to have any kind of consistency in the product.

246
00:14:35,380 --> 00:14:37,160
These businesses desperately needed

247
00:14:37,161 --> 00:14:40,200
a lifeline to survive.

248
00:14:40,201 --> 00:14:43,500
It came first in the form of ice.

249
00:14:43,501 --> 00:14:48,900
Ice as an industry flourished in the summer.

250
00:14:48,901 --> 00:14:52,740
A gentleman by the name of Frederick Tudor decided that he

251
00:14:52,741 --> 00:14:56,760
could make money by actually harvesting ice, storing it, and

252
00:14:56,761 --> 00:15:00,260
selling it in warmer parts of either the United States or in

253
00:15:00,261 --> 00:15:04,860
even some other countries. Harvesting ice became big

254
00:15:04,861 --> 00:15:08,760
business. Every pond, lake, and river that froze over during the

255
00:15:08,761 --> 00:15:16,040
winter fed this growing industry. Ice houses, some looking like crude warehouses,

256
00:15:16,041 --> 00:15:21,720
others resembling palaces, were built to store the millions of tons of ice being harvested each

257
00:15:21,721 --> 00:15:22,421
winter.

258
00:15:22,422 --> 00:15:28,280
Usually they would use things like sawdust or straw in between the blocks of ice so that

259
00:15:28,281 --> 00:15:29,777
they wouldn't stick together.

260
00:15:29,778 --> 00:15:33,080
You could store ice for an entire season all the way through the

261
00:15:33,081 --> 00:15:37,620
summertime. The public developed an insatiable

262
00:15:37,621 --> 00:15:41,940
appetite for ice. It was used primarily to preserve

263
00:15:41,941 --> 00:15:46,900
food. But an assassin's bullet would

264
00:15:46,901 --> 00:15:52,360
create a new demand for ice. On July 2nd, 1881, President James

265
00:15:52,361 --> 00:15:55,291
Garfield was mortally wounded by a gunman.

266
00:15:55,292 --> 00:15:58,640
Doctors feared the oppressive heat would destroy

267
00:15:58,641 --> 00:16:03,240
of saving his life. They searched for a way to cool

268
00:16:03,241 --> 00:16:07,180
his room. Navy engineers assembled an ingenious

269
00:16:07,181 --> 00:16:11,380
device using fans and ice. A giant fan sucked hot air from

270
00:16:11,381 --> 00:16:14,980
the outdoors. Overhead was a cistern filled

271
00:16:14,981 --> 00:16:19,200
with shaved ice and water. A pipe carried that cool,

272
00:16:19,201 --> 00:16:24,400
melting water to a case containing 36 terrycloth rollers.

273
00:16:24,401 --> 00:16:28,140
The warm air from outdoors blew across these cold water

274
00:16:28,141 --> 00:16:32,300
soaked rollers, creating chilled air that fed into the president's

275
00:16:32,301 --> 00:16:35,660
room. It was a very simple system,

276
00:16:35,661 --> 00:16:38,460
but it happened to work very well for the purposes.

277
00:16:38,461 --> 00:16:42,460
Unfortunately, the president did die of complications from being

278
00:16:42,461 --> 00:16:46,140
shot. In his death was born a dream

279
00:16:46,141 --> 00:16:49,740
of mass marketing room cooling machines.

280
00:16:49,741 --> 00:16:53,180
That is, if engineers could find an alternative to the four tons of ice

281
00:16:53,181 --> 00:16:57,340
needed each day to operate a cooling device like the one used by the

282
00:16:57,341 --> 00:16:59,740
president.

283
00:16:59,741 --> 00:17:03,420
You have to remember that early on we didn't have electricity.

284
00:17:03,421 --> 00:17:07,420
We didn't have an easy way to be able to produce a mechanical

285
00:17:07,421 --> 00:17:08,121
effect.

286
00:17:08,122 --> 00:17:10,780
For that reason, the first comfort cooling that really was

287
00:17:10,781 --> 00:17:15,100
applied was mostly to larger buildings where we would use a

288
00:17:15,101 --> 00:17:19,660
steam engine to drive a fan, use that fan then to blow air over

289
00:17:19,661 --> 00:17:23,340
blocks of ice and distribute that within a building itself.

290
00:17:23,341 --> 00:17:27,100
Certainly, we couldn't put a large steam driven fan behind a

291
00:17:27,101 --> 00:17:31,900
pile of ice for somebody's house. The breakthrough came with the

292
00:17:31,901 --> 00:17:35,500
discovery of creating ice artificially using mechanical

293
00:17:35,501 --> 00:17:41,500
refrigeration. Engineers found that chemicals such as

294
00:17:41,501 --> 00:17:45,260
methyl chloride and ammonia had cooling properties, which could

295
00:17:45,261 --> 00:17:50,380
be used to lower water temperature. Sealed containers were

296
00:17:50,381 --> 00:17:54,060
assembled for holding water. Compressors powered by steam

297
00:17:54,061 --> 00:17:59,660
engines were hooked up to pipes running into the containers.

298
00:17:59,661 --> 00:18:03,260
The refrigeration chemicals were piped into these containers and

299
00:18:03,261 --> 00:18:06,860
worked in much the same way as current refrigeration chemicals

300
00:18:06,861 --> 00:18:11,260
to lower temperatures below freezing and turn water into ice.

301
00:18:11,261 --> 00:18:15,020
Unlike melting ice from a pond, you don't have any of the scum or

302
00:18:15,021 --> 00:18:18,860
any of the impurities. This was nice and clean.

303
00:18:18,861 --> 00:18:22,060
The same technology used for making ice was applied to

304
00:18:22,061 --> 00:18:26,660
cooling rooms. And in 1901, engineer Alfred Wolfe

305
00:18:26,661 --> 00:18:29,500
designed one of the first mechanical room refrigeration

306
00:18:29,501 --> 00:18:34,900
systems for the New York Stock Exchange.

307
00:18:34,901 --> 00:18:38,300
The refrigerant of choice was ammonia.

308
00:18:38,301 --> 00:18:41,220
It was pumped through pipes that cooled the water inside three

309
00:18:41,221 --> 00:18:46,060
100-ton chillers. The cool air was released through

310
00:18:46,061 --> 00:18:51,820
42 vents hidden inside the ornate ceiling of the Stock Exchange.

311
00:18:51,821 --> 00:18:56,320
And the warm air was vented outside through ducts.

312
00:18:56,321 --> 00:18:59,560
The system worked well, but there were flaws.

313
00:18:59,561 --> 00:19:02,560
The cooled air being fed onto the trading floor was not at a

314
00:19:02,561 --> 00:19:07,600
consistent temperature. Moreover, the level of humidity

315
00:19:07,601 --> 00:19:11,380
inside the Stock Exchange fluctuated.

316
00:19:11,381 --> 00:19:13,380
We're all familiar with the phrase, it's not the heat,

317
00:19:13,381 --> 00:19:16,220
it's the humidity. To be able to take humidity out

318
00:19:16,221 --> 00:19:20,620
of the room is really the thing that makes us much more

319
00:19:20,621 --> 00:19:24,320
comfortable on those hot and muggy days.

320
00:19:24,420 --> 00:19:27,460
Humidity is measured by how much moisture one cubic foot

321
00:19:27,560 --> 00:19:31,460
of air can hold. As temperature is lowered,

322
00:19:31,560 --> 00:19:35,060
moisture condenses out of the air.

323
00:19:35,160 --> 00:19:38,880
So at 92 degrees, a cubic foot of air can hold 16 grains of

324
00:19:38,980 --> 00:19:41,980
water. But at 31 degrees, it can hold

325
00:19:42,080 --> 00:19:47,580
only two grains of water. The inspiration for solving

326
00:19:47,581 --> 00:19:51,220
the humidity problem came to Willis Carrier in an uncanny

327
00:19:51,221 --> 00:19:55,420
manner, when in 1902, the young engineer was waiting for a

328
00:19:55,421 --> 00:19:58,220
train. While waiting for his train

329
00:19:58,221 --> 00:20:03,020
on the platform, fog rolled in. And it was during this time

330
00:20:03,021 --> 00:20:07,140
where this dense fog came in that he had a flash of genius.

331
00:20:07,141 --> 00:20:09,940
In that moment, Carrier realized that the key to

332
00:20:09,941 --> 00:20:13,380
controlling humidity in a room was to be able to replicate

333
00:20:13,381 --> 00:20:17,580
fog-like conditions inside a mechanical refrigeration system.

334
00:20:17,581 --> 00:20:24,340
Since the source of air used for cooling a building comes from the outdoors, the temperature

335
00:20:24,341 --> 00:20:30,100
fluctuates, and so does its humidity. Carrier designed a machine that had tiny

336
00:20:30,101 --> 00:20:35,600
water sprays in it, so as the outside air was drawn in, it would be saturated with water

337
00:20:35,601 --> 00:20:41,020
at a temperature where it could hold 100% humidity.

338
00:20:41,021 --> 00:20:45,680
Since that saturated air would now have a fixed amount of humidity in it, applying principles

339
00:20:45,681 --> 00:20:50,740
of physics, when it was cooled by the system and released into the building, it would always

340
00:20:50,741 --> 00:20:53,422
contain a precise amount of humidity.

341
00:20:53,423 --> 00:20:57,480
When that air mixed back into the warm, humid air of the

342
00:20:57,481 --> 00:21:03,903
building, it effectively cooled and lowered the humidity of the building's air.

343
00:21:03,904 --> 00:21:04,960
He called his

344
00:21:04,961 --> 00:21:07,755
device the Apparatus for Treating Air.

345
00:21:07,756 --> 00:21:11,580
It was patented in 1906 and became the forerunner of

346
00:21:11,581 --> 00:21:14,820
air conditioning systems. The apparatus for treating air

347
00:21:14,821 --> 00:21:19,680
was well received, particularly in industries where humidity was

348
00:21:19,681 --> 00:21:24,360
a big factor in manufacturing. Known as the father of air

349
00:21:24,361 --> 00:21:28,340
conditioning, Carrier set up his own company in 1907 and mass

350
00:21:28,341 --> 00:21:32,700
produced systems for everything from factories to hotels.

351
00:21:32,701 --> 00:21:35,880
However, most Americans could only experience the comforts of

352
00:21:35,881 --> 00:21:41,580
air conditioning at the movies. Throughout the early 1910s,

353
00:21:41,581 --> 00:21:44,080
many movie theaters closed in the summer months.

354
00:21:47,580 --> 00:21:51,580
That all changed in 1917, when the Central Park Theater in

355
00:21:51,581 --> 00:21:55,580
Chicago became the first fully air-conditioned theater.

356
00:21:55,581 --> 00:21:59,580
Others followed, and by the early 1920s, Americans were

357
00:21:59,581 --> 00:22:04,580
rushing to the movies. For many years, air

358
00:22:04,581 --> 00:22:07,580
conditioning and heating were two distinctly separate units.

359
00:22:07,581 --> 00:22:10,580
They were eventually combined to share the same thermostat.

360
00:22:10,581 --> 00:22:19,380
thermostat, coils, and ducts. By the late 1920s and early

361
00:22:19,381 --> 00:22:24,740
1930s, air conditioning had come into the American home.

362
00:22:24,741 --> 00:22:28,080
Freon replaced methyl chloride and ammonia, which were found

363
00:22:28,081 --> 00:22:32,120
to be highly toxic if they leaked out.

364
00:22:32,121 --> 00:22:35,480
Early home air conditioners were huge room units modeled after

365
00:22:35,481 --> 00:22:38,680
refrigerators. You probably felt the heat

366
00:22:38,681 --> 00:22:40,480
coming out of the back of the refrigerator

367
00:22:40,481 --> 00:22:41,620
or from underneath.

368
00:22:41,621 --> 00:22:45,820
What we do is we take heat in a refrigerator

369
00:22:45,821 --> 00:22:47,920
out of the inside of the box, and we

370
00:22:47,921 --> 00:22:50,320
move the heat to the outside.

371
00:22:50,321 --> 00:22:53,920
It wasn't too much of a leap to take that type of a system

372
00:22:53,921 --> 00:22:57,820
and say, OK, we can not only cool an icebox with that,

373
00:22:57,821 --> 00:23:03,200
but we also can take that and actually cool a room.

374
00:23:03,201 --> 00:23:04,680
Some of the early air conditioners

375
00:23:04,681 --> 00:23:08,680
wheels and could be moved from room to room.

376
00:23:08,681 --> 00:23:12,680
The warm air was vented out the window through tubes.

377
00:23:12,681 --> 00:23:15,680
These portable systems were later replaced with a fixed window

378
00:23:15,681 --> 00:23:20,680
unit that's still popular today. Air conditioning was

379
00:23:20,681 --> 00:23:24,680
marketed as an essential component of modern living.

380
00:23:24,681 --> 00:23:27,680
Manufacturers claimed it promoted better sleeping and

381
00:23:27,681 --> 00:23:30,680
eating and healthier, dust-free homes.

382
00:23:30,681 --> 00:23:33,680
Americans bought the pitch and became a nation of air

383
00:23:33,681 --> 00:23:40,680
conditioning addicts. Cool your entire home with

384
00:23:40,681 --> 00:23:44,680
trained central air conditioning. In the early 1950s,

385
00:23:44,681 --> 00:23:47,680
central air conditioning first appeared in homes.

386
00:23:47,681 --> 00:23:51,680
And with it, architecture forever changed.

387
00:23:51,681 --> 00:23:54,680
Before air conditioning, homes were designed to stay as cool

388
00:23:54,681 --> 00:23:59,680
as possible, using overhanging eaves for shade, opposing windows

389
00:23:59,681 --> 00:24:03,280
for cross ventilation, and porches, which provided refuge

390
00:24:03,380 --> 00:24:05,080
from the heat of a home on a summer day.

391
00:24:08,180 --> 00:24:10,520
When central air conditioning came into the home,

392
00:24:10,620 --> 00:24:14,820
these architectural cooling devices slowly vanished.

393
00:24:14,920 --> 00:24:18,540
But air conditioning did more than just change the home.

394
00:24:18,640 --> 00:24:21,740
Probably the bigger thing that air conditioning really did

395
00:24:21,840 --> 00:24:26,140
was allow you to start building and have a lot of expansion

396
00:24:26,240 --> 00:24:27,980
in areas where it's a lot warmer.

397
00:24:27,981 --> 00:24:31,480
Air conditioning really is the thing that created the entire

398
00:24:31,481 --> 00:24:34,620
business structure that exists in the South.

399
00:24:34,621 --> 00:24:37,560
And with business came people.

400
00:24:37,561 --> 00:24:41,960
It affected the migration of the US population,

401
00:24:41,961 --> 00:24:44,300
changing it from the South to the North,

402
00:24:44,301 --> 00:24:47,500
to the North to the South.

403
00:24:47,501 --> 00:24:51,040
Air conditioning fueled the growth of America's Sun Belt.

404
00:24:51,041 --> 00:24:53,900
It became cool to live in places like Las Vegas,

405
00:24:53,901 --> 00:24:57,900
Atlanta, Miami, Phoenix, and Houston.

406
00:24:57,901 --> 00:25:00,500
They all existed before air conditioning, but they were

407
00:25:00,501 --> 00:25:03,200
outposts. They weren't the big population

408
00:25:03,201 --> 00:25:07,540
centers and the big business centers that we think of today.

409
00:25:07,541 --> 00:25:10,960
As business centers shifted and grew, so did the design of the

410
00:25:10,961 --> 00:25:15,660
buildings people worked in. The office buildings of an

411
00:25:15,661 --> 00:25:19,500
earlier time were decidedly uncool.

412
00:25:19,501 --> 00:25:23,060
During the 1800s and early 1900s, they stood only a few

413
00:25:23,061 --> 00:25:27,460
stories high. They often featured spacious courtyards

414
00:25:27,461 --> 00:25:32,560
and windows that opened to try to enhance air circulation.

415
00:25:32,561 --> 00:25:36,000
Many buildings were decked out in awnings from top to bottom in

416
00:25:36,001 --> 00:25:39,820
an attempt to provide shade. During the winter, heat was

417
00:25:39,821 --> 00:25:44,320
provided by large boilers and furnaces in the basement.

418
00:25:44,321 --> 00:25:47,660
But in the summer, working in an office building was a test of

419
00:25:47,661 --> 00:25:51,020
endurance. If you are working and all you're

420
00:25:51,021 --> 00:25:52,760
is sweating in an office building.

421
00:25:52,860 --> 00:25:55,960
It really hurts people's productivity.

422
00:25:56,060 --> 00:25:58,120
Not to mention morale.

423
00:25:58,220 --> 00:26:00,060
As the day progressed, it would become

424
00:26:00,160 --> 00:26:02,360
hotter and hotter inside.

425
00:26:02,460 --> 00:26:03,860
The humidity would be rising.

426
00:26:03,960 --> 00:26:06,460
Pretty soon, you were sticking to your desk.

427
00:26:06,560 --> 00:26:08,780
Yes, you had your handy table fan sitting there,

428
00:26:08,880 --> 00:26:12,580
blowing air over you, but it wasn't doing much good.

429
00:26:12,680 --> 00:26:14,780
Central air conditioning came to the rescue

430
00:26:14,880 --> 00:26:17,020
in the late 1920s.

431
00:26:17,120 --> 00:26:19,780
The essential impact on high-rise buildings

432
00:26:19,781 --> 00:26:24,520
is that it allowed us to enclose the building.

433
00:26:24,620 --> 00:26:30,520
We no longer were dependent on open windows or ventilation.

434
00:26:30,620 --> 00:26:34,520
In fact, high-rises became towers of glass.

435
00:26:34,620 --> 00:26:36,940
One of the first to be erected was the United Nations

436
00:26:37,040 --> 00:26:40,940
Building, built in 1952.

437
00:26:41,040 --> 00:26:44,340
We could design a building with lots of windows in it

438
00:26:44,440 --> 00:26:48,140
because we didn't have to worry so much about how much heating

439
00:26:48,141 --> 00:26:51,040
occur in the summertime, and we didn't have to worry about how

440
00:26:51,041 --> 00:26:53,880
much cooling effect we would have in the wintertime from all

441
00:26:53,881 --> 00:26:55,880
of those windows.

442
00:26:55,881 --> 00:27:00,380
We no longer had to rely on secondary things, such as

443
00:27:00,381 --> 00:27:04,380
awnings, to shield a window from the sun coming in and making

444
00:27:04,381 --> 00:27:07,300
the room extremely hot.

445
00:27:07,301 --> 00:27:09,900
Air conditioning made it possible to work longer hours

446
00:27:09,901 --> 00:27:12,700
in summer and helped increase productivity.

447
00:27:18,140 --> 00:27:20,980
When cars were invented during the late 1800s,

448
00:27:21,080 --> 00:27:25,480
absolutely no thought was given to climate control.

449
00:27:25,580 --> 00:27:27,140
When it was hot, they'd be hot.

450
00:27:27,240 --> 00:27:28,580
When it was cold, they'd be cold.

451
00:27:28,680 --> 00:27:31,480
But that's what motoring was about back in those days.

452
00:27:31,580 --> 00:27:34,080
Their open-air design made them totally impractical

453
00:27:34,180 --> 00:27:35,900
in bad weather.

454
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:39,700
Automobiles did not find extensive use

455
00:27:39,800 --> 00:27:41,900
during winter months.

456
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:45,100
Around Thanksgiving time, people

457
00:27:45,101 --> 00:27:49,700
People used to take their automobiles off the road,

458
00:27:49,800 --> 00:27:55,040
put it on the cinder blocks, and store away for winter months.

459
00:27:55,140 --> 00:27:58,340
And they would bring out the automobiles again

460
00:27:58,440 --> 00:28:00,840
in springtime.

461
00:28:00,940 --> 00:28:02,220
Those that did brave the weather

462
00:28:02,320 --> 00:28:03,880
sought protection from the elements

463
00:28:03,980 --> 00:28:07,960
underneath a huge rubberized cloth called a storm apron.

464
00:28:10,700 --> 00:28:15,100
Around 1908, closed-body cars were introduced.

465
00:28:15,101 --> 00:28:18,240
So were some strange ways of heating them.

466
00:28:18,340 --> 00:28:21,700
Drivers and passengers warmed stones in their home fireplace,

467
00:28:21,800 --> 00:28:26,640
wrapped them in cloth, and kept them on their laps.

468
00:28:26,740 --> 00:28:28,940
The alternatives were not much better.

469
00:28:29,040 --> 00:28:33,160
Some tried to take the heat of the engine's exhaust

470
00:28:33,260 --> 00:28:35,660
and route it into the passenger compartment.

471
00:28:35,760 --> 00:28:38,460
Not a very good idea what with exhaust

472
00:28:38,560 --> 00:28:41,760
being the pollutants they are.

473
00:28:41,860 --> 00:28:44,860
Not to mention how toxic those fumes are.

474
00:28:44,861 --> 00:28:48,800
In the early days of motoring, several deaths occurred.

475
00:28:48,900 --> 00:28:55,500
So in response to this concern, heater engineers developed what

476
00:28:55,600 --> 00:28:59,000
are called the hot water heaters.

477
00:28:59,100 --> 00:29:02,420
Introduced in 1926, the hot water heater

478
00:29:02,520 --> 00:29:05,420
was mounted under the dashboard.

479
00:29:05,520 --> 00:29:07,320
The heat of the engine warmed water

480
00:29:07,420 --> 00:29:11,320
that passed through a maze of small tubes just behind a fan

481
00:29:11,321 --> 00:29:18,320
air through the car. These hot water heaters were

482
00:29:18,321 --> 00:29:22,160
extremely effective and extremely popular.

483
00:29:22,161 --> 00:29:27,320
In fact, nothing changed for 10 years until 1937, when auto

484
00:29:27,321 --> 00:29:30,320
manufacturer Nash stirred excitement with what would

485
00:29:30,321 --> 00:29:35,320
become the model for all other car heaters.

486
00:29:35,321 --> 00:29:39,320
Just a twirl of the dial of the weather I control transforms

487
00:29:39,321 --> 00:29:42,120
Transforms January into June.

488
00:29:42,121 --> 00:29:44,860
The Weather Eye operated in much the same way as a home

489
00:29:44,861 --> 00:29:48,320
heating system, drawing fresh outside air into the engine

490
00:29:48,321 --> 00:29:52,560
compartment, where it was heated by the engine, filtered, and

491
00:29:52,561 --> 00:29:57,280
released inside the car. There was a price to pay for this

492
00:29:57,281 --> 00:30:00,680
newfound level of comfort. For years, heating was a high

493
00:30:00,681 --> 00:30:05,680
ticket luxury item. During the 50s and even into the

494
00:30:05,681 --> 00:30:11,680
60s, heaters were likely to be an extra cost option.

495
00:30:11,681 --> 00:30:16,160
By the 70s, heaters were pretty much a standard feature.

496
00:30:16,161 --> 00:30:17,920
Not air conditioning.

497
00:30:17,921 --> 00:30:20,660
For decades, cooling a car was of far less concern

498
00:30:20,661 --> 00:30:23,260
than heating it.

499
00:30:23,261 --> 00:30:26,800
Early cars were open top and provided fresh air.

500
00:30:26,801 --> 00:30:28,460
And even after cars were enclosed,

501
00:30:28,461 --> 00:30:30,800
people seemed happy to just roll down a window

502
00:30:30,801 --> 00:30:34,100
and catch a breeze.

503
00:30:34,101 --> 00:30:36,860
We used to have what we called 440 air conditioning,

504
00:30:37,560 --> 00:30:40,120
four windows down, 40 miles an hour.

505
00:30:40,420 --> 00:30:43,780
And that's what people drove around with for years and years

506
00:30:43,781 --> 00:30:44,481
and years.

507
00:30:44,960 --> 00:30:47,220
There were a few attempts at comfort cooling.

508
00:30:47,980 --> 00:30:50,080
Some manufacturers installed vents.

509
00:30:51,260 --> 00:30:54,180
